# Portfolio guidelines

Recruiters are hiring the person, not the game. Follow these when writing or editing the site. Reference: https://hugo.fyi/

## Contributions

- Every project lists what Yen-Chun did. Edit those bullets only in `contributions.js`.
- A bullet states the result, not only the task. "Designed level 3" is weak. "10-level puzzle where the soda bottle is the only way up" is the bar.
- On a team project, write his part only. Do not present the studio's work as his.
- Junior, in progress, or a jam that did not place is fine. Do not inflate.

## Shape of a project

- Lead with his work. The game gets one short line, then the bullets.
- No long paragraphs and no long video as the pitch. Prefer a still or a short loop, a header, and bullets.
- Awards and nominations sit on the card, visible without opening the project. Edit them in `contributions.js` under `awards`. Mento-Issue: Pittsburgh non-traditional award, Global Game Jam 2025. Camp Movewell (ReNUSHU team): 2026 Games for Change nominee, Best in Health & Wellness. A nomination stays a nomination.
- Do not attach GDDs or process writeups.

## The page

- Work should be visible without hunting through buttons. The LED wall can stay; the project grid shows every project at once, one click away.
- Layout has to hold on phone and tablet, not only a desktop.
- Proofread. Blurbs still have typos: challange, desinged, Gererousity, controling.

## Leave out

- Creative-vision language in place of the skill.
- A thin prototype dressed up as a finished game. One small finished game beats a pile of unnamed prototypes.
